  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonably dry and temperature airmass (perhaps\na couple degrees below normal) will be ushered in behind this\nmorning's cold front as it pushes further offshore. In the near\nterm, low stratus will continue to plague eastern NC as moisture\nremains pooled under the frontal inversion. Stronger surge of\ndry air, combined with mixing after daybreak, should usher in\nsunny skies. The high will become the dominant weather feature\nfor the weekend and much of next week. Cooler temperatures with\nhighs in the 60s prevail today and tomorrow before a slight\nwarm-up next week into the low 70s, although humidity will\nremain low. We will have to watch for the risk of some frost\nover the upcoming nights, particularly tonight and Mon night as\nlows dip towards the upper 30s and skies remain clear and winds\nlight. Will defer to day shift to determine whether advisories\nare needed.\n\nBeyond mid-week most guidance depicts an increasingly unsettled\npattern as complex mid-level low deepens over the eastern CONUS.\nThere is at least some signal for a developing low off our coast\nbeyond Wednesday, although its evolution and timing remain\nhighly uncertain.",
